next-auth-proxy.md:
## Why it exists
Logged-in versus logged-out pages is the most common requirement and the most common source of security bugs. The key idea: a redirect improves the user experience, but **the real check must sit next to the data**.

{{dg:0}}

## Layer 1: proxy.ts (formerly middleware)
In Next.js 16, `proxy.ts` replaced `middleware.ts` and runs on the Node.js runtime. It is a good place to refresh sessions and redirect anonymous users early.
```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const hasSession = request.cookies.has('session')
  if (!hasSession && request.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith('/dashboard')) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
  }
  return NextResponse.next()
}

export const config = { matcher: ['/dashboard/:path*'] }
```
Migrating? Rename `middleware.ts` to `proxy.ts` and the exported function to `proxy`.

## Layer 2: a data access layer
Put every authorization decision in one server-only module that all pages, actions and route handlers call.
```ts
// lib/auth.ts
import 'server-only'
import { cache } from 'react'
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'
import { createClient } from '@/lib/supabase/server'

export const requireUser = cache(async () => {
  const supabase = await createClient()
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.getClaims()
  if (error || !data?.claims) redirect('/login')
  return { id: data.claims.sub as string }
})
```
```tsx
// app/dashboard/page.tsx
export default async function Dashboard() {
  const user = await requireUser()           // verified on the server, every time
  const todos = await getTodosFor(user.id)   // plus RLS in the database
  // render
}
```
`cache` from React de-duplicates the check within a single request, and `server-only` makes the build fail if the module is ever imported into client code.

## Layer 3: the database
Row Level Security means that even if a check is missed in code, the database still refuses to return another user's rows. See the Supabase RLS lesson.

## Why three layers
| Layer | Catches | Cannot catch |
|---|---|---|
| proxy.ts | Anonymous users on protected URLs, expired sessions | Routes the matcher misses, Server Actions called directly |
| Data access layer | Every page, action and handler that calls it | A new route someone forgets to protect |
| RLS | Missed checks in code | Business rules RLS cannot express |

> [!security] In March 2025 a middleware authorization bypass (CVE-2025-29927) showed that relying on middleware alone for access control is unsafe. Always verify close to the data.
> [!pitfall] Deciding access from a session read out of the cookie trusts an unverified token. Use getClaims() or getUser().
> [!pitfall] Matchers are easy to get wrong: a route outside the matcher is simply unprotected. Do not make the matcher your only defense.

next-rendering.md:
## Why it exists
Classic React apps ship a big JavaScript bundle and fetch data in the browser, so users wait for code, then data, then paint. The **App Router** in Next.js renders as much as possible on the server and sends HTML plus a compact description of the component tree. Pages appear sooner and less JavaScript reaches the browser.

{{dg:0}}

## Server and Client Components
By default every component in the App Router is a **Server Component**: it runs only on the server, can be `async`, can read the database directly, and never ships its code to the browser. Add `'use client'` at the top of a file to make it a **Client Component**, which can use state, effects and browser APIs. The `'use client'` line is a boundary: everything the file imports becomes client code too.

## Worked example
```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx  (Server Component)
import { AddToCart } from './add-to-cart'

export default async function Page() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/products')   // runs on the server
  const products: { id: string; name: string }[] = await res.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {products.map(p => (
        <li key={p.id}>{p.name} <AddToCart id={p.id} /></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```
```tsx
// app/products/add-to-cart.tsx  (Client Component)
'use client'
import { useState } from 'react'

export function AddToCart({ id }: { id: string }) {
  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false)
  return <button onClick={() => setAdded(true)}>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to cart'}</button>
}
```
Only the small button ships as JavaScript. The list and the data fetching stay on the server.

## Dynamic routes and Server Actions
```tsx
// app/products/[id]/page.tsx
export default async function Product({ params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }) {
  const { id } = await params            // params is async in Next.js 15 and 16
  // load the product with this id
}
```
```ts
// app/products/actions.ts
'use server'
export async function addReview(formData: FormData) {
  const text = String(formData.get('text') ?? '')
  // authenticate, validate, then write to the database
}
```
A **Server Action** is a function that runs on the server and can be called from a form or a client component without writing an API route.

## Streaming
Wrap slow parts in `<Suspense fallback={...}>` (or add a `loading.tsx` file) and Next.js sends the fast parts immediately, then streams the slow parts in when they are ready.

## Choosing
| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Read data and render markup | Server Component |
| onClick, useState, useEffect, browser APIs | Client Component |
| Change data from a form or button | Server Action |
| Public JSON endpoint or webhook | Route Handler (`route.ts`) |

> [!pitfall] Marking a high-level layout as a client component pulls everything under it into the client bundle. Push the boundary down to the smallest interactive component.
> [!pitfall] You cannot pass functions or class instances from a Server Component to a Client Component as props. Pass plain serializable data.
> [!security] Server Actions are public HTTP endpoints. Authenticate and validate inside every action, not just in the UI.
